The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
for (i = 0; i < MMUv2_MAX_STLB_ENTRIES; i++, buf += SZ_4K)
if (v2_context->mtlb_cpu[i] & MMUv2_PTE_PRESENT)
```
Reason:
The loop increments the buffer pointer (`buf`) for each entry in the `mtlb_cpu` array, regardless of whether the entry is valid (i.e., whether it contains a present Page Table Entry). This means that if there are no valid PTEs, the buffer pointer will still be incremented, leading to an invalid pointer being used in the subsequent `memcpy` operation.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
for (i = 0; i < MMUv2_MAX_STLB_ENTRIES; i++)
if (v2_context->mtlb_cpu[i] & MMUv2_PTE_PRESENT) {
    buf += SZ_4K;
}
```
Reason:
The fix separates the increment of the buffer pointer from the loop iteration. The buffer pointer is only incremented when a valid PTE is found, ensuring that `buf` points to a valid memory location when `memcpy` is called. This prevents memory corruption or crashes by ensuring that only valid entries are copied into the coredump buffer.

